.\" xargs.1 -- maux's xargs(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text, not copied from a
.\" historic xargs(1)), so it carries no third-party notice --
.\" MIT like the code, same posture as cut.1. Rendered at build
.\" time to build/man/XARGS.TXT and staged on the play image as
.\" /usr/man/XARGS: `more /usr/man/XARGS`.
.TH XARGS 1 "October 8, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
xargs \- construct argument lists and run a utility with them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xargs
[
.B \-0pt
] [
.BI \-n " number"
] [
.BI \-s " size"
] [
.B \-t
] [
.BI \-I " replstr"
] [
.I utility
[
.I argument
] ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Xargs
reads whitespace- and newline-delimited arguments from the
standard input, appends as many of them as will fit to the
given
.I argument
list, and runs
.I utility
with the result.  This is repeated until standard input is
exhausted.
.I Utility
defaults to
.BR echo .
If standard input carries no arguments at all,
.I utility
is not run and the exit status is 0.
.PP
Arguments are delimited by unquoted blanks (space or tab) or
newlines; adjacent delimiters collapse to one.
.B '...'
and
.B \(dq...\(dq
each protect everything but their own matching quote (not a
bare newline, and not the shell's own escaping -- backslash is
literal inside either kind of quote).  An unquoted backslash
escapes the very next byte, newline included.  A quote left
open at end of input is an error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " number"
Use at most
.I number
standard-input-derived arguments per invocation of
.IR utility ,
in addition to any
.I argument
words given on the command line.
.TP
.BI \-s " size"
Bound the standard-input-derived arguments' packed byte total
per invocation to
.I size
bytes.  Both
.B \-n
and
.B \-s
are additionally bounded by this machine's own spawn() ceiling
(the packed argument list, and the pointer array indexing it,
are both fixed-size); a value asking for more than the
ceiling allows is silently lowered to it.  If a single
standard-input argument alone exceeds the size in effect, it
still runs by itself rather than being dropped.
.TP
.B \-t
Write each constructed command line to standard error,
terminated with a newline, just before running it.
.TP
.B \-0
Items end at a NUL byte and at nothing else: blanks, newlines, quotes
and backslashes are ordinary bytes. An empty item is dropped. With
.B \-I
a NUL ends a line and nothing is stripped from it.
.TP
.B \-p
Before each invocation write the command line to standard error, then
.B ?...
and read one reply line from the terminal (the standard error, which
stays the console when the standard input is a file or a pipe). The
utility runs only if the reply begins with y or Y; anything else,
including an empty line, skips that invocation without an error. Where
standard error is not a terminal nobody can be asked and every
invocation is skipped. Replaces
.BR \-t .
.TP
.BI \-I " replstr"
Read one whole input line per invocation (leading blanks
stripped, blank lines skipped) and substitute every occurrence
of
.I replstr
in
.I utility
and its
.I argument
words with that line's text.  Disables
.BR \-n .
.SH FILES
/usr/man/XARGS  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
find(1), sh(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if every invocation of
.I utility
exited 0, or if none ran; 123 if some invocation exited between
1 and 125 (noted, but xargs keeps going); 124 if some invocation
exited abnormally (this machine's wait status cannot always
distinguish an exit(255) from a kill by signal, so both fold
into this one code, and xargs stops); 126 if
.I utility
was found but could not be invoked; 127 if
.I utility
could not be found on
.BR PATH ;
1 for a usage error, an unmatched quote, or an argument list
too long to leave any room for standard input's own arguments.
.SH BUGS
.B \-0
(NUL-delimited input),
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-r ,
.B \-x
and
.B \-P
are not implemented.  The child's standard input is closed
(not connected to a terminal, and not xargs's own standard
input, which POSIX leaves unspecified for this reason: xargs's
own input is very often the read end of a pipe xargs itself is
still draining).  xargs's own environment is not passed to
.IR utility ,
unlike a real execvp -- a deliberate simplification that keeps
the whole packed-argument budget available to argument text.
Each argument's own text is truncated at 255 bytes.
